  "slug": "exhibit-senescence-like-secretory-5528",
  "summary": "Recent preprints link fibroblast cAMP, quiescent secretory programs and macrophage vesicles to how injured tissues resolve and remodel.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread across these preprints is how cell-intrinsic signaling and secreted factors govern resolution of injury and tissue remodeling, though not every item shares this focus. Fibroblast-specific loss of Gs-dependent cAMP production blocked spontaneous resolution of lung and skin fibrosis after bleomycin injury, identifying endogenous cAMP as required for fibrotic recovery. Quiescent human fibroblasts and preadipocytes display a secretory program (QASP) that overlaps the senescence SASP and varies with induction method and oxygen. Extracellular vesicles from alternatively activated macrophages reduce necrosis and promote liver repair after acetaminophen injury, alongside increased TREM2 macrophages. The CSP5 peptide, a truncated Cav1 scaffolding congener, reduces lung injury, fibrosis and mucus expression in mouse models. What is new is that quiescent cells adopt a senescence-like secretory state, and that endogenous cAMP production is needed for fibrosis to resolve.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-22"
}
